Abstract_Text:
Advancing sustainability requires more than institutional commitments, technological advances and economic resources. Social values, attitudes and beliefs shape how individuals and communities perceive sustainability challenges, what they consider important, and how they respond to societal and policy changes. They provide an important social foundation for public support, collective action and behavioural change. Yet these orientations are neither uniform across societies nor static over time, raising important questions about how they shape, reflect and respond to sustainable development.
Understanding and tracking social values is therefore important for examining the social conditions that enable or constrain sustainability transitions. Existing research has investigated values and attitudes associated with specific domains, including environmental protection, climate action, gender equality, health and social trust. Broader conceptual frameworks and measurement approaches have also sought to capture multidimensional patterns of social values. However, important questions remain about how these orientations differ across countries, social groups and generations, how they evolve over time, and how they interact with broader socioeconomic and institutional changes and sustainability outcomes.
This session invites theoretical, empirical and methodological contributions examining social values in relation to sustainability, particularly from cross-national, longitudinal and comparative perspectives. We welcome research focusing on specific sustainability domains, individual value dimensions, relationships and trade-offs among different priorities, as well as comprehensive approaches to measuring and understanding multidimensional social values. Relevant topics include, but are not limited to:
• Cross-national and longitudinal patterns of social values, attitudes and sustainability priorities;
• Development and validation of indicators, composite indices and measurement frameworks;
• Relationships between social values, sustainability outcomes, policy support and behavioural change;
• Socioeconomic, demographic, cultural and institutional differences in value orientations;
• Intergenerational value change and broader processes of social transformation;
• Competing sustainability priorities, public engagement and implications for sustainability governance.

Recognition of the circular economy in the Sustainable Development Goals has remained indirect: SDG 12 addresses sustainable consumption and production, but no goal, target, or headline indicator squarely addresses material use and circularity. Meanwhile global material extraction exceeds 100 billion tonnes per year, and global circularity is declining, threatening climate, biodiversity, and resource-security objectives. As the post-2030 agenda process is starting, this interactive session asks how circular economy and sustainable material use can be anchored more firmly – and why that matters from three complementary perspectives: business practice, science and government.
Co-chaired by the International Sustainable Development Research Society and the Dutch Ministry of Economic Affairs and Climate, the session opens with a keynote on the state of global resource use and the post-2030 window of opportunity. A short panel then presents three perspectives: how bottom-up business action is already transforming value chains and what is holding them back; what science-based international governance of material use could look like; and why governments need explicit circularity goals and mechanisms to steer national policy and international collaboration effectively;
Participants then join one of four round tables, each hosted by a panel member, to debate concrete anchoring options: goals and indicators, governance instruments, implementation and review, and coalitions. Each table distils its discussion into two to three key messages, which are prioritized in a plenary wrap-up. The session concludes with a short set of "recommendations on materials and circularity" for the conference's input to the 2027 UN SDG Summit and post-2030 process, supported by business, scientists and governments alike.
